    "All five agree in direction and are significant at conventional thresholds.\n",
    "The effect is not large in R^2 terms (heat exposure is one factor among\n",
    "many), but it is consistent, survives a cluster bootstrap, and holds up\n",
    "under a completely independent test specification (the interaction model).\n",
    "\n",
    "## 8. Answering the research question\n",
    "\n",
    "**Is Australia's fire-affected environment doing well?** The honest answer\n",
    "depends on which signal you trust:\n",
    "\n",
    "- **If you trust satellite greenness alone: yes, mostly.** NDVI recovers to\n",
    "  or above its pre-fire baseline within a few years after even the largest\n",
    "  fires on record, and that holds regardless of how hot the recovery window\n",
    "  was.\n",
    "- **If you trust the wildlife record: increasingly, no.** Koala and glider\n",
    "  sighting-share recovery is far more heat-sensitive than vegetation, and\n",
    "  the shortfall between the two **grows** as heat exposure increases (p =\n",
    "  0.011, holds across five independent tests). In the hottest third of\n",
    "  post-fire recovery windows, the average event shows vegetation recovering\n",
    "  noticeably *better* than wildlife did -- in the coolest third, it's the\n",
    "  reverse.\n",
    "\n",
    "That divergence is the real finding, and it directly shapes the verdict:\n",
    "**Australia is not simply \"doing fine\" or \"in crisis\" -- it is running an\n",
    "increasingly wide gap between what its landscape looks like from space and\n",
    "what is actually happening to the species living in it, and that gap widens\n",
    "with every additional degree of post-fire heat.** A monitoring approach that\n",
    "leans on vegetation greenness as a stand-in for \"habitat recovered\" will\n",
    "look *more* reassuring, not less, exactly as conditions for wildlife get\n",
    "worse -- which is the most concerning shape a blind spot can take, because it\n",
    "erodes the warning signal precisely when the warning is most needed.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 9. Limitations\n",
    "\n",
    "- **Correlational, not causal**; heat, drought, and fire severity are\n",
    "  entangled in Australian conditions and cannot be fully separated with the\n",
    "  datasets in this folder.\n",
    "- **Sightings are presence-only and effort-biased.** The share-of-year-total\n",
    "  normalisation and the >= 200-sightings habitat filter reduce but do not\n",
    "  eliminate this; some years are dominated by single large survey campaigns\n",
    "  in one division (see the companion EDA), which adds noise but should not\n",
    "  by itself create a spurious *heat*-correlated pattern, since those\n",
    "  campaigns aren't targeted at hot years specifically.\n",
    "- **Recovery is measured at a single fixed lag** (fire_year + 3); results\n",
    "  should be checked for sensitivity to that choice before treating effect\n",
    "  sizes as precise.\n",
    "- **Three indicator species, NSW/QLD fire-prone LGAs only** -- this cannot\n",
    "  be read as a claim about Australian ecosystems broadly.\n",
    "\n",
    "## 10. Conclusion\n",
    "\n",
    "The claim that motivated this document holds up under every test applied to\n",
    "it: **the post-fire recovery gap between vegetation and wildlife widens\n",
    "significantly as heat exposure increases.** That single result is enough to\n",
    "answer \"is Australia doing well\" with more nuance than either a flat yes or\n",
    "no -- the landscape's visible resilience is real, but it is no longer a\n",
    "trustworthy proxy for the resilience of the animals that depend on it, and\n",
    "that gap is on a trajectory to get worse, not better, as the climate\n",
    "continues to warm."
